Patch 48. After patch 47 (and 46, which touches other files).

The command processor's thread polls in two places, and both kept a core busy by yielding in a
loop. On Torchlight (POCO X7, a fight, null GPU plugin, simpleperf over 25 s) the "GPU Commands"
thread used about a third of one core, almost all of it in sched_yield and clock_gettime; packet
work was a few percent.

- An empty ring buffer: the worker yielded 500 times before each 5 ms wait on
  write_ptr_index_event_. It now yields kSpinYields (16) times. No wakeup can be lost by waiting
  sooner: the event is auto-reset and UpdateWritePointer sets it after every write, so a write
  between the check and the wait leaves it set. CallInThread now sets it too when it queues a
  function, which before waited for the spin or the 5 ms timeout.
- WAIT_REG_MEM, the condition not met: with vsync off it yielded on every poll; with vsync on it
  slept wait / 0x100 whole milliseconds per poll (yielding when that was 0). It now yields
  kSpinYields times, then sleeps 20 us, doubling up to a cap: the packet's poll interval
  (wait * 1000 / 0x100 us) or 500 us, whichever is smaller, and with vsync on at least the whole
  milliseconds it slept before (Windows' rex::thread::Sleep turns anything under 1 ms into a
  yield, so a smaller cap would spin there). A cap under 20 us (wait under 6) keeps yielding.
  PrepareForWait and ReturnFromWait are called as before. On Windows the sleeps under 1 ms are
  ::Sleep(0) or SwitchToThread, so there only the vsync-on sleeps of 1 ms or more remain real
  sleeps, as before.

Nothing the guest waits on is skipped: every poll still reads the register or memory, and the
worker still runs every packet. What can change is latency: a condition met during a sleep is
seen up to the cap later; with vsync on that is never later than before (the first polls come
sooner than the old fixed sleep), with vsync off up to 500 us (before, at once). The desktop
platforms change too: where Sleep is a real sleep (Linux, macOS), the vsync-off polls and short
vsync-on polls now sleep instead of yielding.

Test: no unit test reaches these loops (the command processor needs a graphics system); the
SDK's tests pass with it. Torchlight on the POCO X7 (2026-10-22, combat bench, A B B A, every
run started at thermal status 0, CPU 34.7-37.5 C, big cores at 2500 MHz throughout): the "GPU
Commands" thread's share of the app's cycles (simpleperf, 25 s of fight) went from 9.49 to
2.83 % with fps_cap 0 and from 3.42 to 2.24 % with fps_cap 60 and vsync. Guest frame times,
presented frame rate and CPU temperature did not change beyond the runs' spread (guest p50
18.6-21.4 ms either way); in 5-minute fights (fps_cap 60) the mean battery current was -414 mA
with it against -445 mA without, inside the spread of the runs (-375 to -474 mA).

